+  "title": "GOV-0111: WP-2026-0117 — content-assert the references pinned only by filename, and restore the assertion spliced into a comment (#216 + #230 + #210 as one WP)",
+  "problem": "🧠 **ARCHI (BARCHI-3)** — WP-UAT-A-style freeze not applicable; this is a remediation WP. Clustering **#216 + #230 + #210** into one governed work package: `WP-2026-0117` / `BADF-DEM-0104`.\n\n## Why one WP and not three\n\nThree issues, **one defect at three addresses**: a reference or an assertion pinned by a **proxy** rather than by its property.\n\n- **#216** — four `badf-engineering-verification` references pinned by filename only.\n- **#230** — six of `badf-build`'s fourteen references carry no content assertion.\n- **#210** — an assertion spliced into a trailing comment, so the `allowed_tools` guard runs for nobody.\n\nThree separate PRs would each re-sign `badf/lockfile.json` and therefore conflict with the other two by construction; every landing would cost the others a rebase plus a full G07 regeneration — the structural cost three seats measured independently tonight. This follows BADF-QA's batching rule (*batch tooling findings once the class is characterized*) rather than the one-issue-one-task default. **The deviation is deliberate and flagged**, and was endorsed by SARCHI as coordinator before work began.\n\n## Ownership, recorded because it matters for author≠reviewer\n\n- **#216** is mine — the fix existed on an unpushed branch since before this session. I flagged it twice as \"must not be lost\" and then silently deprioritised it for the G10 program. **That is a lapse, disclosed rather than quietly cleared.**\n- **#230** is BADF-QA's finding, **ceded explicitly** on the grounds that they intend to review the fix and holding both roles would make them author and reviewer.\n- **#210** is unclaimed; BARCHI-2 confirmed `badf-build` was idle-not-self-assigned and gave a courtesy yes on touching their family.\n\n## Measurement — two independent instruments, agreeing\n\nThe six unpinned `badf-build` references were established twice, by different methods:\n\n```\nempirical:  gut each reference, run the contract module, observe green   (BARCHI-3)\nstatic:     which filenames reach the read() helper at line 39           (BADF-QA)\nboth:       acceptance · evidence-packaging · handoff-to-g08\n            preflight · retry-and-budget · stop-conditions      -- 6 of 14, exact\n```\n\nAgreement carries information **because the instruments differ**; two runs of either would have carried none.\n\n## The rule this WP records, with its precondition\n\n> **Pin the value where the file is the source of truth; pin the structure where the value is owned and guarded elsewhere.**\n> A structure-only anchor detects **deletion**, never **weakening** — *\"a threshold is stated\"* is satisfied by `max_attempts: 999999`.\n\nStructure-anchoring is correct for all six here **only because none of them owns a threshold**: `stop-conditions` lists conditions, `preflight` asks *\"budget remaining?\"* with no figure, and `retry-and-budget`'s `1/2/3` is a worked illustration whose own text defers to the Work Package's `execution_budget` — guarded in code at `badf_gate.py` IMP-C3 / IMP-I11. BADF-QA's test, adopted with the precondition attached, because the next family may not be so convenient.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- All fourteen `badf-build` references and the four `badf-engineering-verification` references redden their module when emptied, **each observed red**, with the already-asserted eight probed as a negative control.\n- `allowed_tools` is a live assertion again for `badf-engineering-verification`, red-verified by setting it to `['Bash']`.\n- Anchors pin structure only; **no reference content is edited** — this WP changes tests, not doctrine documents.\n- No gate, schema, or lifecycle change.\n\n## Deferred, not fixed (Review notes become issues)\n\nBADF-QA observed that a reader can mistake `retry-and-budget.md`'s illustrative `1/2/3` for normative. Fixing that means **editing a frozen reference's prose**, which is outside a test-only WP's scope. Filed as its own issue rather than absorbed here.\n\n## Not claimed\n\nThat this closes the class. The remedy pins the tokens it names and nothing else, and other families are unprobed. Per BADF-REV's **direction test**, vacuity risk lives where a glob-derived collection is the *iteration source*, not where it is the set being checked against — `REFERENCE_ANCHORS` is a fixed literal map, so it is on the safe side **by construction rather than by luck**, which is a materially different claim from having anticipated it.\n\n**Version tier:** patch (tests only). **Lean mode:** advisory. Shared-surface across two families → QA + REV by BADF-QA and BADF-REV.\n\n<!-- agent: architect -->\n",
+  "authorized_by": {
+    "principal": "operator",
+    "principal_type": "human"
+  },
+  "recorded_at": "2026-08-31T09:30:00Z",
+  "provenance": "EXPORTED_FROM_SOURCE",
+  "status": "AUTHORIZED",
+  "note": "WP-2026-0117: cluster remediation of one defect at three addresses -- a reference or an assertion pinned by a PROXY rather than its property. #216 (four badf-engineering-verification references pinned by filename only), #230 (six of badf-build's fourteen with no content assertion), #210 (self.assertEqual([], allowed_tools) spliced into a trailing comment, guard dark). One WP rather than three because three PRs would each re-sign the lockfile and conflict by construction -- BADF-QA's batching rule, deviation from one-issue-one-task flagged and endorsed by SARCHI. The six were measured TWO independent ways (empirical gutting; static read()-call analysis) agreeing exactly. Records the rule: pin the value where the file is the source of truth, the structure where the value is owned and guarded elsewhere -- a structure-only anchor detects deletion, never weakening. Deferred to #254: retry-and-budget.md's illustrative 1/2/3 reading as normative, not fixed in-PR because it edits frozen prose."
+}
